<%# The card upstream's example builds: a link that, held under the pointer for
    a moment, explains what it points at.

    The trigger is `as: :a` — a hover card usually hangs off something already
    in the text — and it stays focusable, because Radix opens on focus as well
    as on hover (hover-card.tsx:138-141) and that is the only way a keyboard
    reaches it at all.

    Nothing inside the card is reachable by keyboard: the controller takes every
    tabbable in it out of the tab order, which is Radix's own behaviour and
    follows from the card having no way to be tabbed *into*. Put nothing in one
    that is only available there. %>
<div class="flex h-64 items-start justify-center pt-12">
  <%= render(Shadcn::HoverCard::Component.new) do |card| %>
    <% card.with_trigger(as: :a, href: "#", class: "text-sm font-medium underline underline-offset-4") do %>
      @shadcn
    <% end %>
    <% card.with_hover_card_content do %>
      <div class="flex gap-4">
        <%= render(Shadcn::Avatar::Component.new) do |avatar| %>
          <% avatar.with_fallback { "SC" } %>
        <% end %>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold">@shadcn</h4>
          <p class="text-sm">The React framework — created and maintained by @vercel.</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">Joined December 2021</p>
          <%# A link, on purpose: it is what the tab-order behaviour has to act
              on, and a card with nothing focusable in it would make that
              assertion vacuous. It is also the realistic case — the reason to
              build a card rather than a tooltip is that it can hold one. %>
          <a href="#" class="text-xs font-medium underline underline-offset-4">View profile</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
